1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,763
At the Corner Cafe, someone typed the word
three into Asha's score file, instead of

2
00:00:05,783 --> 00:00:06,630
the number 3.

3
00:00:06,667 --> 00:00:08,965
The score board prints one line.

4
00:00:08,985 --> 00:00:12,008
Then a wall of red text, and nothing else.

5
00:00:12,028 --> 00:00:14,472
The staff have no idea what to do.

6
00:00:15,087 --> 00:00:17,329
This is Code Quest, Chapter 22.

7
00:00:17,349 --> 00:00:20,687
Today, your programs learn to handle
problems.

8
00:00:20,733 --> 00:00:24,958
And an AI assistant will promise code that
never crashes.

9
00:00:24,978 --> 00:00:26,373
It will sound sure.

10
00:00:26,393 --> 00:00:28,085
Your tests will decide.

11
00:00:28,687 --> 00:00:34,175
Here is today's mission: the same score
board, now ready for bad data, with clear

12
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:34,787
messages.

13
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:37,549
And a missing file gets a message too.

14
00:00:37,569 --> 00:00:39,781
Let's build it, piece by piece.

15
00:00:40,383 --> 00:00:43,463
Catch an error, and show a clear message
instead.

16
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:45,310
Use else and finally.

17
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:47,523
Catch the specific error you expect.

18
00:00:47,567 --> 00:00:50,037
Raise your own error, and test it.

19
00:00:50,067 --> 00:00:53,836
And ask an AI what could go wrong, then
test each case.

20
00:00:54,417 --> 00:00:59,454
An exception is an error that happens
while a program runs, like a value error,

21
00:00:59,474 --> 00:01:00,607
or file not found.

22
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:03,851
You have read exception reports since
Chapter 9.

23
00:01:03,871 --> 00:01:07,493
Now your program catches them, and decides
what to do.

24
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:10,389
Customers type words instead of numbers.

25
00:01:10,409 --> 00:01:11,683
Files get deleted.

26
00:01:11,703 --> 00:01:15,564
Good programs expect this, and carry on
when they can.

27
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:18,861
A try block runs code that might fail.

28
00:01:18,881 --> 00:01:24,826
If an exception happens inside it, Python
stops that block, and jumps to a matching

29
00:01:24,846 --> 00:01:25,760
except block.

30
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,840
The except line names the exception it
catches.

31
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:33,852
An exception of another type is not
caught, and the program stops, as before.

32
00:01:34,453 --> 00:01:35,933
Try always runs first.

33
00:01:35,953 --> 00:01:38,113
It stops at the first exception.

34
00:01:38,133 --> 00:01:43,013
Except value error runs only when a value
error happened in try.

35
00:01:43,033 --> 00:01:47,193
Else runs only when try finished with no
exception at all.

36
00:01:47,233 --> 00:01:50,709
And finally runs always, last, whatever
happened.

37
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:53,932
Think of the safety net under a trapeze.

38
00:01:53,952 --> 00:01:59,000
The act goes on as planned, and the net
catches the artist only after a fall.

39
00:01:59,033 --> 00:02:01,189
But a real net catches anyone.

40
00:02:01,209 --> 00:02:04,597
Except catches only the type of error it
names.

41
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:05,930
Quick check.

42
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:07,693
When does the else block run?

43
00:02:19,047 --> 00:02:19,188
C.

44
00:02:19,208 --> 00:02:21,928
Only when try raised no exception.

45
00:02:21,948 --> 00:02:24,830
Finally is the one that always runs.

46
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:25,957
Breather.

47
00:02:25,977 --> 00:02:28,680
Four parts, and you know when each one
runs.

48
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:31,459
Next, Asha catches her first error.

49
00:02:36,183 --> 00:02:36,640
Step 1.

50
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:41,616
In Chapter 4, int of the word two stopped
the program with a value error.

51
00:02:41,636 --> 00:02:45,433
Line 2 holds three answers, and the loop
tries each one.

52
00:02:45,467 --> 00:02:49,373
Line 5 tries to turn the answer into a
whole number.

53
00:02:49,393 --> 00:02:50,657
Line 6 prints it.

54
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:56,622
If line 5 fails, Python skips the rest of
the try block, and jumps to the except

55
00:02:56,642 --> 00:02:57,811
block on line 7.

56
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:10,144
Only line 8.

57
00:03:10,164 --> 00:03:13,015
Line 5 failed, so line 6 never ran.

58
00:03:13,035 --> 00:03:16,460
And the loop went on with the next answer.

59
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:20,852
Int ignores the spaces around 3, as you
saw in Chapter 4.

60
00:03:20,872 --> 00:03:25,607
And exclamation r, from Chapter 20, shows
the quotation marks.

61
00:03:26,183 --> 00:03:26,741
Step 2.

62
00:03:26,761 --> 00:03:28,390
Line 7 reads a file.

63
00:03:28,410 --> 00:03:31,193
That can fail with file not found.

64
00:03:31,233 --> 00:03:33,813
Lines 8 and 9 catch it, with a message.

65
00:03:33,833 --> 00:03:38,044
Else, on line 10, runs only when the file
was read.

66
00:03:38,064 --> 00:03:41,113
Finally, on line 12, runs every time.

67
00:03:41,133 --> 00:03:45,682
Lines 16 and 17 call it twice: with week,
and with month.

68
00:03:57,047 --> 00:03:58,791
No scores yet, then done.

69
00:03:58,811 --> 00:04:03,237
The except block ran, else was skipped,
and finally ran anyway.

70
00:04:03,267 --> 00:04:07,027
For week, else printed the file, and
finally printed done.

71
00:04:07,067 --> 00:04:08,877
Keep the try block small.

72
00:04:08,897 --> 00:04:12,246
Only the line that can fail belongs inside
it.

73
00:04:12,817 --> 00:04:13,342
Step 3.

74
00:04:13,362 --> 00:04:18,407
In Chapter 7, a while true loop asked
until the answer was valid.

75
00:04:18,427 --> 00:04:20,667
Now try sits inside the loop.

76
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:25,080
A word gives a value error, and line 7
asks for a whole number.

77
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:28,960
If the number is from 0 to 4, break ends
the loop.

78
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:31,832
Otherwise line 11 explains the range.

79
00:04:43,213 --> 00:04:45,642
Please type a number from 0 to 4.

80
00:04:45,662 --> 00:04:48,684
Seven is a whole number, so no exception.

81
00:04:48,704 --> 00:04:52,023
But the if on line 9 catches the wrong
value.

82
00:04:52,067 --> 00:04:54,792
Try catches an answer that is not a
number.

83
00:04:54,812 --> 00:04:57,217
If catches a number outside the range.

84
00:04:57,237 --> 00:04:59,771
And each message says what to type next.

85
00:05:00,387 --> 00:05:00,965
Breather.

86
00:05:00,985 --> 00:05:03,627
A word no longer crashes Asha's program.

87
00:05:03,667 --> 00:05:08,003
Next, Asha's own functions learn to refuse
bad input.

88
00:05:12,750 --> 00:05:13,237
Step 4.

89
00:05:13,257 --> 00:05:16,351
A function can refuse bad input with
raise.

90
00:05:16,371 --> 00:05:21,130
Line 4 creates a value error, with a
message that names the price.

91
00:05:21,167 --> 00:05:22,603
Line 11 catches it.

92
00:05:22,623 --> 00:05:26,897
As error keeps the exception in a variable
called error.

93
00:05:26,933 --> 00:05:29,190
Line 12 prints that message.

94
00:05:40,547 --> 00:05:44,203
Not saved: price cannot be negative: minus
1.

95
00:05:44,223 --> 00:05:47,717
The message from line 4 arrives at line
12.

96
00:05:47,767 --> 00:05:50,993
A clear message names the problem and the
value.

97
00:05:51,013 --> 00:05:53,631
Error, with nothing else, helps nobody.

98
00:05:54,253 --> 00:05:54,789
Step 5.

99
00:05:54,809 --> 00:05:58,441
Line 7 is a bare except: no error type at
all.

100
00:05:58,461 --> 00:06:03,363
It catches every exception, even the ones
your own bugs cause.

101
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:05,248
Look closely at line 5.

102
00:06:16,613 --> 00:06:17,859
It is refused too!

103
00:06:17,879 --> 00:06:19,969
Line 5 says anser, not answer.

104
00:06:19,989 --> 00:06:22,711
That name error was caught, and hidden.

105
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:25,993
Now line 7 catches value error only.

106
00:06:26,013 --> 00:06:29,060
The spelling mistake is no longer hidden.

107
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:33,265
The report names line 5, and even suggests
the right name.

108
00:06:33,285 --> 00:06:36,295
Catch the errors you expect from the data.

109
00:06:36,315 --> 00:06:37,955
Let your own bugs show.

110
00:06:38,553 --> 00:06:39,271
Quick check.

111
00:06:39,291 --> 00:06:43,141
Which except line can hide a spelling
mistake in your own code?

112
00:06:54,513 --> 00:06:54,634
B.

113
00:06:54,654 --> 00:06:55,759
The bare except.

114
00:06:55,779 --> 00:06:58,782
It catches everything, including your
bugs.

115
00:06:58,802 --> 00:07:03,282
It even catches Control C, the keys you
press to stop a program.

116
00:07:03,883 --> 00:07:04,411
Step 6.

117
00:07:04,431 --> 00:07:10,598
Asha keeps with tax in her cafe tools
module, with the new check on lines 4 and

118
00:07:10,618 --> 00:07:10,754
5.

119
00:07:11,353 --> 00:07:14,784
In Chapter 19, tests checked return
values.

120
00:07:14,804 --> 00:07:16,710
Line 11 checks an error.

121
00:07:16,730 --> 00:07:20,963
The test passes only if line 12 raises a
value error.

122
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,361
Both tests pass: two dots.

123
00:07:23,953 --> 00:07:25,968
Here is an older with tax.

124
00:07:25,988 --> 00:07:29,803
Lines 5 and 6 have no check for a negative
price.

125
00:07:29,833 --> 00:07:33,853
The test on lines 9 to 11 is the same as
before.

126
00:07:45,213 --> 00:07:45,413
No.

127
00:07:45,433 --> 00:07:49,073
The function returned a value instead of
refusing.

128
00:07:49,093 --> 00:07:52,147
Pytest reports: did not raise value error.

129
00:07:52,753 --> 00:07:53,296
Breather.

130
00:07:53,316 --> 00:07:55,673
You can catch, raise, and test errors.

131
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:57,380
Now, back to the mission.

132
00:08:02,117 --> 00:08:03,477
Back to the mission.

133
00:08:03,497 --> 00:08:06,790
Here is the score file, with Omar's
damaged row.

134
00:08:07,387 --> 00:08:09,300
Line 4 starts a counter.

135
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:13,167
The outer try, on lines 5 and 6, opens the
file.

136
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:18,660
Inside the loop, a small try on lines 8
and 9 protects one row at a time.

137
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:22,380
A bad score prints a message that names
the row and the value.

138
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:25,700
A good score is printed and counted, in
else.

139
00:08:25,733 --> 00:08:29,031
Lines 15 and 16 handle a missing file.

140
00:08:29,051 --> 00:08:31,214
Line 17 prints the count.

141
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:42,698
2.

142
00:08:42,718 --> 00:08:48,489
Omar's row is skipped with a message, and
Lina still loads, because the try protects

143
00:08:48,509 --> 00:08:49,730
one row at a time.

144
00:08:49,767 --> 00:08:54,559
Now Asha wants this as a function, load
scores, for the quiz app.

145
00:08:54,579 --> 00:08:58,705
She builds it with an AI assistant, one
stage at a time.

146
00:08:59,283 --> 00:09:03,663
This chapter's AI skill: ask what could go
wrong in your code.

147
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:06,990
Then make one test case for each problem,
and run it.

148
00:09:07,033 --> 00:09:09,150
And never paste private data.

149
00:09:09,170 --> 00:09:13,938
A real file of staff names is private, so
paste a made-up sample.

150
00:09:14,553 --> 00:09:15,603
Stage 1: plan.

151
00:09:15,623 --> 00:09:21,106
Asha asks what could go wrong, and what
the program should do each time.

152
00:09:21,126 --> 00:09:22,023
No code yet.

153
00:09:22,067 --> 00:09:27,213
The AI lists three problems: a missing
file, a score that is not a number, and a

154
00:09:27,233 --> 00:09:28,893
file with only the header.

155
00:09:29,487 --> 00:09:30,570
Stage 2: write.

156
00:09:30,590 --> 00:09:36,457
Asha writes her own load scores first, and
asks which lines handle each problem.

157
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:38,884
The AI names lines 12 to 15.

158
00:09:38,904 --> 00:09:41,287
That matches what she meant.

159
00:09:41,887 --> 00:09:43,261
Stage 3: generate.

160
00:09:43,281 --> 00:09:48,917
To compare, Asha asks the AI for the same
function, from a short request.

161
00:09:48,967 --> 00:09:53,438
The AI is sure: it handles every problem,
and never crashes.

162
00:09:54,053 --> 00:09:58,007
Look where the try starts, on line 7, and
where it ends.

163
00:09:58,027 --> 00:10:01,483
Lines 11 and 12 catch everything, and do
nothing.

164
00:10:01,533 --> 00:10:06,988
Line 17 loads the damaged file: Asha, Omar
with three, and Lina.

165
00:10:18,347 --> 00:10:18,549
No.

166
00:10:18,569 --> 00:10:19,738
Only Asha loads.

167
00:10:19,758 --> 00:10:25,678
The try block holds the whole loop, so
Omar's row stopped it, and Lina was lost,

168
00:10:25,698 --> 00:10:26,867
with no message.

169
00:10:26,900 --> 00:10:32,348
A better request names the exceptions to
catch, and asks for a small try block.

170
00:10:32,953 --> 00:10:33,974
Stage 4: test.

171
00:10:33,994 --> 00:10:39,843
Asha asks for test cases, each with the
file and the number of scores it should

172
00:10:39,863 --> 00:10:40,363
return.

173
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:41,691
The AI lists three.

174
00:10:41,711 --> 00:10:44,726
They match the three problems from the
plan.

175
00:10:45,320 --> 00:10:48,720
She runs all three cases on the AI's
loader.

176
00:10:48,740 --> 00:10:52,761
The damaged row case fails: it found 1
score, not 2.

177
00:10:53,353 --> 00:10:58,433
Asha shows the AI the failing case, and
asks for the cause, then a fix.

178
00:10:58,467 --> 00:11:04,618
The AI explains: the whole loop was inside
try, and the bare except hid the error.

179
00:11:04,638 --> 00:11:10,413
The fix puts a small try around one row,
the same idea as Asha's own version.

180
00:11:11,020 --> 00:11:14,566
She runs all three cases again, on the
fixed loader.

181
00:11:14,586 --> 00:11:17,720
All three pass, and Omar's row gets a
message.

182
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:19,414
Stage 5: debug.

183
00:11:19,434 --> 00:11:24,610
In a new test file, one row says only Ben,
with no comma and no score.

184
00:11:24,630 --> 00:11:27,060
Her own version has the same gap.

185
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:27,905
Type error!

186
00:11:27,925 --> 00:11:32,478
The report points at line 11 of the
loader: int of row score.

187
00:11:33,087 --> 00:11:36,647
Asha pastes the error, and asks for the
cause first.

188
00:11:36,667 --> 00:11:42,670
Ben's row has no score, so the value is
None, and int of None raises a type error.

189
00:11:42,690 --> 00:11:48,619
The fix catches both errors as a tuple:
the two names inside round brackets, like

190
00:11:48,639 --> 00:11:50,603
the tuples from Chapter 13.

191
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:55,698
She adds the short row as case 4, and runs
every case again.

192
00:11:55,718 --> 00:11:56,747
All four pass.

193
00:11:57,353 --> 00:11:58,816
Stage 6: improve.

194
00:11:58,836 --> 00:12:01,433
Asha asks for one improvement.

195
00:12:01,467 --> 00:12:06,997
The AI suggests: except Exception, so no
future error can ever crash it.

196
00:12:07,033 --> 00:12:11,695
Exception, with a capital E, is the parent
of almost every error type.

197
00:12:11,715 --> 00:12:16,175
So except Exception catches nearly every
error, your bugs included.

198
00:12:16,787 --> 00:12:18,337
Asha tests the idea.

199
00:12:18,357 --> 00:12:23,755
She adds a spelling mistake on purpose:
line 10 says scor, not score.

200
00:12:23,775 --> 00:12:25,797
Line 11 catches Exception.

201
00:12:25,833 --> 00:12:30,052
Line 16 loads the normal file, with two
good rows.

202
00:12:41,413 --> 00:12:41,594
No.

203
00:12:41,614 --> 00:12:45,931
Every row is skipped with a wrong message,
and the list is empty.

204
00:12:45,951 --> 00:12:47,132
The bug is hidden.

205
00:12:47,753 --> 00:12:53,140
With her specific except, the key error
stops the program, and points at line 10.

206
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:55,743
She finds the spelling mistake at once.

207
00:12:55,763 --> 00:12:57,945
So she keeps the specific except.

208
00:12:58,553 --> 00:12:59,323
Quick check.

209
00:12:59,343 --> 00:13:01,166
A score loader reads a file.

210
00:13:01,186 --> 00:13:03,930
Which except should catch a damaged score?

211
00:13:15,313 --> 00:13:15,439
D.

212
00:13:15,459 --> 00:13:16,316
Value error.

213
00:13:16,336 --> 00:13:19,310
That is the error a damaged score raises.

214
00:13:19,330 --> 00:13:21,282
Let every other error show.

215
00:13:21,887 --> 00:13:22,550
Breather.

216
00:13:22,570 --> 00:13:27,487
The AI made two confident mistakes, and
Asha's tests caught both.

217
00:13:27,533 --> 00:13:31,386
Next, four mistakes beginners make with
try and except.

218
00:13:36,117 --> 00:13:36,906
Mistake 1.

219
00:13:36,926 --> 00:13:37,554
You try.

220
00:13:37,574 --> 00:13:41,359
Line 4 turns the word four into a whole
number.

221
00:13:41,379 --> 00:13:43,302
Line 5 catches an error.

222
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:54,880
No.

223
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:58,613
Int of four raises a value error, not a
type error.

224
00:13:58,633 --> 00:14:00,590
So the program still stops.

225
00:14:00,633 --> 00:14:03,621
The fix: catch the type the report names.

226
00:14:03,641 --> 00:14:06,629
Run the code once without try, to see it.

227
00:14:07,220 --> 00:14:07,941
Mistake 2.

228
00:14:07,961 --> 00:14:11,054
Line 4 failed, so score was never created.

229
00:14:11,074 --> 00:14:13,500
Line 7 still uses it: name error.

230
00:14:13,533 --> 00:14:17,812
The fix: put the code that needs score in
else.

231
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:19,130
Mistake 3.

232
00:14:19,150 --> 00:14:20,881
Remember the four parts.

233
00:14:20,901 --> 00:14:23,435
Look at what follows the try block.

234
00:14:34,813 --> 00:14:36,869
An except or a finally block.

235
00:14:36,889 --> 00:14:39,660
Every try needs one, straight after it.

236
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:42,022
Python cannot even read the file.

237
00:14:42,620 --> 00:14:43,389
Mistake 4.

238
00:14:43,409 --> 00:14:46,153
Raise needs an exception, not text.

239
00:14:46,173 --> 00:14:48,600
Python stops with a type error.

240
00:14:48,633 --> 00:14:51,872
The fix: put the message inside a value
error.

241
00:14:52,487 --> 00:14:53,493
Last breather.

242
00:14:53,513 --> 00:14:57,087
Errors are now part of your plan, not a
surprise.

243
00:14:57,133 --> 00:15:00,322
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

244
00:15:05,050 --> 00:15:05,469
First.

245
00:15:05,489 --> 00:15:09,716
In the ask again program, a player types
3.5 as the score.

246
00:15:09,736 --> 00:15:14,182
Which message do they see, and how would
you make it clearer?

247
00:15:25,547 --> 00:15:28,791
Try it after the video, and write your new
message.

248
00:15:29,387 --> 00:15:29,808
Second.

249
00:15:29,828 --> 00:15:34,474
When is it better to stop the whole
program, instead of skipping a damaged

250
00:15:34,494 --> 00:15:34,727
row?

251
00:15:34,747 --> 00:15:36,997
Think about a file of bank payments.

252
00:15:48,347 --> 00:15:49,500
Keep your answer.

253
00:15:49,520 --> 00:15:51,847
It is a design choice, not a rule.

254
00:15:52,417 --> 00:15:53,162
Let's recap.

255
00:15:53,182 --> 00:15:56,987
Try and except catch an exception, and
show a clear message.

256
00:15:57,033 --> 00:15:59,455
Else runs when nothing failed.

257
00:15:59,475 --> 00:16:01,083
Finally runs always.

258
00:16:01,133 --> 00:16:03,314
Catch the exception you expect.

259
00:16:03,334 --> 00:16:05,443
A bare except hides your bugs.

260
00:16:05,467 --> 00:16:07,517
Keep each try block small.

261
00:16:07,567 --> 00:16:10,517
Raise refuses bad input, with a message.

262
00:16:10,567 --> 00:16:13,850
And pytest raises tests that the error
happens.

263
00:16:14,453 --> 00:16:15,784
Mission complete!

264
00:16:15,804 --> 00:16:19,201
You earned 100 XP and the Safety Net
badge.

265
00:16:21,450 --> 00:16:22,982
Now it is your turn.

266
00:16:23,002 --> 00:16:25,930
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

267
00:16:25,967 --> 00:16:31,075
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including an order that asks again until

268
00:16:31,095 --> 00:16:31,907
it is valid.

269
00:16:31,933 --> 00:16:34,685
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

270
00:16:34,705 --> 00:16:39,330
In the last one, you fix an AI's menu
loader that loses items.

271
00:16:39,917 --> 00:16:41,832
Asha's scores are safe now.

272
00:16:41,852 --> 00:16:44,627
But a C S V row holds only flat values.

273
00:16:44,647 --> 00:16:49,357
Her quiz needs questions, answers and
settings, inside each other.

274
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:55,084
In Chapter 23, you will save structured
data as JSON, and read data that other

275
00:16:55,104 --> 00:16:57,570
programs send, through web A P Is.

276
00:16:57,600 --> 00:16:59,372
Do the warm-up tasks now.

277
00:16:59,392 --> 00:17:01,380
They take about ten minutes.

278
00:17:01,400 --> 00:17:03,333
See you in Chapter 23.
